'''Kingston University London''' is a [[Public university|public]] [[research university]] located within the [[Royal Borough of Kingston upon Thames]], in [[South London|South West London]], England. Its roots go back to the '''Kingston Technical Institute''', founded in 1899. It received university status in 1992, before which the institution was known as '''Kingston Polytechnic'''.

Kingston has around 17,000 students and a turnover of £192 million.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/aboutkingstonuniversity/factsandfigures/financialstatements/|title=Financial statements - Facts and figures - Kingston University London|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|language=en|access-date=2019-09-18}}</ref> It has four campuses situated in [[Kingston upon Thames|Kingston]] and [[Roehampton]]. The university specialises in the arts, design, fashion, science, engineering, and business and is organised into four faculties: [[Kingston School of Art]], Faculty of Business and Social Sciences (which combines [[Kingston Business School]] and the School of Law, Social and Behavioural Sciences), Faculty of Health, Science, Social Care and Education, and Faculty of Engineering, Computing and the Environment. The Kingston Business School is [[Council for National Academic Awards|CNAA]] [[Master of Business Administration|MBA]] degree approved. In 2017, the university won ''[[The Guardian]]'' University Award for teaching excellence.<ref name=":0" />  Kingston is a member of the [[European University Association]], the [[Association of Commonwealth Universities]], and [[University Alliance]] group.

==History==
Kingston was founded as Kingston Technical Institute in 1899, it offered courses in chemistry, electrical wiring, construction and nursing. In 1917 Gipsy Hill College for teacher training opened, a predecessor of Kingston University.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gipsy Hill Teacher Training College Archive - Archives Hub |url=https://archiveshub.jisc.ac.uk/data/gb2108-kuas1 |access-date=2023-02-27 |website=archiveshub.jisc.ac.uk}}</ref> Gipsy Hill College was created by [[Belle Rennie]] and led by an Australian named [[Lillian Daphne de Lissa]].<ref>{{Cite ODNB |title=Rennie [née Moorhouse], Isabella Southern [Belle] (1875–1966), educationist |url=https://www.oxforddnb.com/display/10.1093/ref:odnb/9780198614128.001.0001/odnb-9780198614128-e-48580 |access-date=2023-02-27 |date=2004 |language=en |doi=10.1093/ref:odnb/48580}}</ref> In 1930 the [[Kingston School of Art]] separated from the Technical Institute, later to become Kingston College of Art in 1945. In 1946 Gipsy Hill College moved to Kingston Hill. In 1951, the first Penrhyn Road campus buildings opened. Kingston was recognised as a 'Regional College of Technology' by the [[Ministry of Education (United Kingdom)|Ministry of Education]] in 1957. In 1970, the College of Technology merged with the College of Art to become Kingston Polytechnic, offering 34 major courses, of which 17 were at degree level.<ref name=kuwebsite>{{cite web|url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/aboutkingstonuniversity/factsandfigures/ourhistory/|title=Our history - Facts and figures|website=Kingston University Website|access-date=12 October 2014}}</ref> In 1975, Kingston merged with the Gipsy Hill College of Education, incorporating the college's faculty into Kingston's Division of Educational Studies.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://cdn.kingston.ac.uk/documents/aboutkingstonuniversity/factsandfigures/ourhistory/documents/history-of-kingston-text.pdf|title=A History of Kingston university|last=Gibson|first=Michael|date=1999|publisher=Kingston University}}</ref>

Kingston was granted university status under the [[Further and Higher Education Act 1992]]. In 1993, Kingston opened the Roehampton Vale campus building and in 1995, Kingston acquired [[Dorich House]].

In June 2021, Kingston launched its Future Skills campaign, highlighting the importance of skills for innovation and the vital role they play in driving a thriving UK economy. The results of their 2021 and 2022 surveys, conducted with support from [[YouGov]], demonstrated key attributes such as problem-solving, critical thinking, communication, adaptability and creativity remain among the core skills most valued by employers.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/aboutkingstonuniversity/future-skills/|title=Future Skills|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|language=en|access-date=2022-08-16}}</ref>

==Campuses and estate==

===Penrhyn Road===
[[File:Universität Kingston.jpg|thumb|right|Main building, Penrhyn Road campus]]
This is the main university campus located close to Kingston town centre. Students based here study Arts and Social Sciences, Civil Engineering, Computing and Information Systems and Mathematics, Earth Sciences and Geography, Statistics, Biosciences, Pharmacy, Chemistry and Pharmaceutical Science, and Radiography. Development at this site has extended to the Learning Resources Centre. In 2015, the [[Union of Kingston Students]], moved into the main building. Penrhyn Road also houses the refurbished Fitness Centre.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/sport/latest-news/2012/august/fitness-centre/ | title=Kingston University Fitness Centre - Sport and recreation news - Sport and Active Lifestyles - Kingston University London }}</ref>

====Town House====
Kingston University's Town House building was opened in January 2020 and is located on the Penrhyn Road campus. The six-storey building was the first by [[Royal Institute of British Architects]] (RIBA) Gold Medal-winning firm [[Grafton Architects]] in the [[United Kingdom]] and is open to students, staff and the local community.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/news/article/2288/06-jan-2020-flagship-multimillion-pound-town-house-building-opens-at-kingston-university/|title=Flagship multi-million pound Town House building opens at Kingston University|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|date=6 January 2020 }}</ref>

The building work was carried out by [[Hertfordshire]]-based [[Willmott Dixon]]. It features a three-floor academic library, archive, dance studios and a studio theatre. It incorporates a covered internal courtyard, two cafes and external balconies and walkways culminating in a rooftop garden with views across [[Kingston upon Thames (parish)|Kingston upon Thames]] and the River Thames.

Town House has been nominated for several awards, including two ''[[The Guardian]]'' University Awards,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/news/article/2308/13-mar-2020-kingston-university-shortlisted-for-two-guardian-university-awards/|title=Kingston University shortlisted for two Guardian University Awards
|website=www.kingston.ac.uk}}</ref> and a RIBA London Regional Award.<ref name="kingston.ac.uk">{{cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/news/article/2348/07-jul-2020-kingston-universitys-town-house-building-and-kingston-school-of-art-campus-regeneration-shortlisted-for-riba/|title=Kingston University's Town House building and Kingston School of Art campus regeneration shortlisted for RIBA London Regional Awards|website=www.kingston.ac.uk}}</ref> In October 2021, Town House was announced as the winner of the 25th RIBA [[Stirling Prize]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=RIBA Stirling Prize 2021|url=https://www.architecture.com/awards-and-competitions-landing-page/awards/riba-stirling-prize|access-date=2021-10-18|website=www.architecture.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-10-15|title=Student 'Town House' wins Stirling Prize to be named UK's best new building|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/architecture/riba-stirling-prize-2021-kingston-university-london-town-house-b1938726.html|access-date=2021-10-18|website=The Independent|language=en}}</ref> 
In 2022 it won the [[European Union Prize for Contemporary Architecture]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/news/article/2661/26-apr-2022-kingston-universitys-town-house-carries-off-highest-accolade-in-european-architecture-winning-prestigious-eu-mies/|title=Kingston University's Town House carries off highest accolade in European architecture, winning prestigious EU Mies van der Rohe Award|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|date=26 April 2022 |language=en|access-date=2022-04-26}}</ref>

===Kingston Hill===
[[File:Kingston Hill campus.jpg|thumb|Kingston Hill campus, Kingston University]]
Kingston Hill mainly caters to Nursing (adult, child, mental health and learning disability), Education, Business, Music and Social Care. Before 1989, this campus was known as Gipsy Hill.

The Business School moved to a new building on the Kingston Hill Campus in 2012.<ref>{{cite news|author=Kieran Long |url= https://www.standard.co.uk/arts/architecture/look-and-learn-intelligent-design-for-education-7630998.html |title=Look and learn: intelligent design for education - Architecture - Arts |work=Evening Standard|location= London |date=11 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121214002704/http://www.standard.co.uk/arts/architecture/look-and-learn-intelligent-design-for-education-7630998.html|archive-date=14 December 2012|access-date=31 March 2015}}</ref>

===Knights Park===

Located on Grange Road, the Knights Park campus is home to some of the students from Kingston School of Art (KSA) - architecture, art and design students. The campus is built on the northern banks of the [[Hogsmill River]] and opened in 1939.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/faculties/kingston-school-of-art/about/history/|title=A history of Kingston School of Art|website=www.kingston.ac.uk}}</ref>

A £29 million refurbishment of the Mill Street workshops, studios and reception area, was completed in March 2020 and includes a gallery, a social space and an art shop.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/news/article/2309/30-mar-2020-refurbishment-of-kingston-school-of-arts-mill-street-building-shows-vision-for-the-future-of/|title=Refurbishment of Kingston School of Art's Mill Street Building shows vision for the future of art school education
|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|date=30 March 2020
}}</ref> The regeneration project was shortlisted for a [[Royal Institute of British Architects]] (RIBA) London Regional Award.<ref name="kingston.ac.uk"/>

===Roehampton Vale===
The Roehampton Vale campus was opened in 1993 by Sir William Barlow, the president of the [[Royal Academy of Engineering]]. The site is located on Friars Avenue, on the outskirts of Kingston. 
This campus is purpose-built and completely dedicated to the study of engineering (aerospace, automotive, motor vehicle and mechanical engineering).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/aboutkingstonuniversity/location/roehampton-vale-kingston/|title=Roehampton Vale - Location - About Kingston University - Kingston University London|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|access-date=2 August 2020}}</ref>

Facilities on site include a wind tunnel, engineering workshops, a flight simulator, a range of vehicles, a Learjet 25 plane, several large 3D printers, plus automotive and aeronautical learning resources.{{citation needed|date=April 2023}}

=== Reg Bailey Theatre Complex ===
Former church converted into the Kingston [[Drama]] students' base, the Reg Bailey has two theatres with lighting and sound equipment, three rehearsal rooms and a [[costume]] room while its annexed Surrey Club is for [[Dance]] students. The Reg Bailey has been home to such alumni members as [[Ben Barnes (actor)|Ben Barnes]], [[Sam Chan]], [[Mandy Takhar]], [[Alphonsia Emmanuel]], [[Jessie Cave]], [[Laura Harling]] and [[Trevor Eve]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk/mgAi.aspx?ID=13601|title=Your council and democracy: REG BAILEY BUILDING|date=20 October 2009|website=Kingston Council|access-date=4 October 2018}}</ref>

===Tolworth Court Sports Ground===
The university's 55-acre sports ground houses twelve football pitches, two rugby pitches, three cricket squares, one American football pitch, one lacrosse pitch, two netball courts and three tennis courts.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/sport/tolworthcourtsportsground/|title=Tolworth Court sports ground - Sport and Active Lifestyles|date=12 October 2014|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|access-date=6 October 2017}}</ref>

===Galleries and museums===
The Stanley Picker Gallery is the Faculty's exhibition space which is now used to present a variety of research-based projects, fellowships and exhibitions.<ref name="stanleypickergallery.org">{{cite web|url=http://www.stanleypickergallery.org |title=The Stanley Picker Gallery at Kingston University is a public venue dedicated to the research, development, production and presentation of interdisciplinary contemporary art practice |publisher=Stanley Picker Gallery |access-date=11 February 2014}}</ref> In 2003, the Stanley Picker Gallery gave birth to Transitstation,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.transitstation.de |title=transitstation - Exhibition as Event |website=Transitstation.de |access-date=15 November 2012}}</ref> which was created/curated by Stanley Picker Fellow Dagmar Glausnitzer-Smith, and former gallery curator Charles Ryder. In 2003, the Director of Foundation Studies in Art and Design, Paul Stafford, converted a run-down public convenience in Kingston town centre into the Toilet Gallery.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.toiletgallery.org/ |title=育毛剤の人気ランキング情報 |publisher=Toiletgallery.org |access-date=15 November 2012 |archive-date=6 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201206002457/http://toiletgallery.org/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Kingston University runs Dorich House Museum<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/dorich/ |title=Dorich House Museum |publisher=Kingston University |access-date=15 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070416033550/http://www.kingston.ac.uk/dorich/ |archive-date=16 April 2007 |url-status=dead  }}</ref> which houses a huge collection of sculptor [[Dora Gordine]]'s work, and examples of [[Russian Imperial]] art and furniture. Dorich House is also used as a meeting and conference venue.{{citation needed|date=March 2025}}

===Other locations===
Additional to the four main campuses is an administration building: Hind Court on London Road which was home to the office of the Vice-Chancellor until 2018 when they moved to Holmwood House.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://riveronline.co.uk/vice-chancellor-spent-600000-on-his-new-office-space/ | title=Vice-chancellor spent £600,000 on his new office space | date=13 November 2018 }}</ref>

Furthermore, art and design studies students from Kingston School of Art are based at River House, on the High Street in Kingston town centre.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/aboutkingstonuniversity/location/river-house-kingston/ | title=River House - Location - About Kingston University - Kingston University London }}</ref>

==Organisation==
Teaching and research are organised in four faculties.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/faculties/|title=Faculties and schools at Kingston University - Kingston University London|website=www.kingston.ac.uk}}</ref>

===Kingston School of Art===
[[Kingston School of Art]] (KSA) was established as part of Kingston Technical Institute founded in 1899. The School of Art separated from the Technical College in 1930 and left Kingston Hall Road to move to Knights Park in 1939. It became Kingston College of Art in 1945 and merged back with the Technical College to form Kingston Polytechnic in 1970. The Polytechnic later became Kingston University in 1992, under which the school was known as the Faculty of Art, Design and Architecture (FADA) until 2017 when it reverted to its historic name.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/aboutkingstonuniversity/factsandfigures/ourhistory/ |title=Our History |access-date=21 May 2013}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://riveronline.co.uk/knights-park-celebrates-the-launch-of-the-kingston-school-of-art/|title=Knights Park celebrates the launch of the Kingston School of Art {{!}} River Online|website=riveronline.co.uk|date=4 October 2017|language=en-GB|access-date=4 November 2017 |last1=Cherubini |first1=Elena }}</ref>
Kingston School of Art delivers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes of study across three schools:<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/faculties/kingston-school-of-art/study/|title=Study at Kingston School of Art|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|access-date=4 November 2017}}</ref>

The Kingston School of Art runs a number of research centres:
*Contemporary Art Research Centre ("CARC"), a Research Centre within the Department of Fine Art
*Colour Design Research Centre
*Screen Design Research Centre
*Modern Interiors Research Design<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/design/MIRC/ |title=Kingston University - Faculty of Art, Design and Architecture - Modern Interiors Research Centre |publisher=Kingston University |access-date=15 November 2012}}</ref>
*Sustainable Design Research Centre<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/design/research/sustainable.html |title=The Sustainable Design Research Centre|access-date=24 April 2007 |publisher=Kingston University |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070313142605/http://www.kingston.ac.uk/design/research/sustainable.html |archive-date=13 March 2007 }}</ref>
*Centre for the Contemporary Visual & Material Culture
*Curating Contemporary Design Research Group
*Real Estate Research Group
*Fashion Industry Research Centre
*Fire, Explosion and Fluid Dynamics (FEFD)
*Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy ([[CRMEP]])<ref>{{Cite web |title=Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy (CRMEP) - Kingston School of Art – Kingston University |url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/faculties/kingston-school-of-art/research-and-innovation/crmep/ |access-date=2023-08-10 |website=www.kingston.ac.uk |language=en-GB}}</ref>

===Faculty of Business and Social Sciences===
The Faculty of Business and Social Sciences combines Kingston Business School and the School of Law, Social and Behavioural Sciences.

[[Kingston Business School]] (KBS) can be traced back to the 1960s. In 1985, the [[Council for National Academic Awards|CNAA]] approved the school's [[Master of Business Administration]] (MBA) degree and the following year KBS moved to Kingston Hill Campus. The Business School is divided into four departments:<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://business.kingston.ac.uk/about-us|title=Kingston Business School {{!}} Find Out About Our Faculty and Facilities {{!}} Kingston Business School, London|website=business.kingston.ac.uk|access-date=4 November 2017}}</ref>

===Faculty of Health, Science, Social Care and Education===
The Faculty of Health, Science, Social Care and Education was founded in August 2022 and contains courses including nursing, midwifery, social work and teacher training education. The faculty also contains life sciences and chemistry and pharmacy courses, which were previously part of Kingston's former Faculty of Science, Engineering and Computing – now the Faculty of Engineering, Computing and the Environment.

The new Faculty of Health, Science, Social Care and Education was established after a formal partnership between Kingston University and [[St George's, University of London]] (SGUL) was mutually terminated in July 2022 after 26 years of working together training the next generation of healthcare workers, social workers and teachers.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/faculties/faculty-of-health-social-care-education/|title=Faculty of Health, Science, Social Care and Education|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|date=15 August 2022|language=en-GB|access-date=15 August 2022}}</ref> The faculty is based at the University's Kingston Hill and Penrhyn Road campuses in Kingston upon Thames.

===Faculty of Engineering, Computing and the Environment===
The Faculty of Engineering, Computing and the Environment was founded in August 2022 and contains courses including mechanical, civil and aerospace engineering, computing, cyber security, quantity surveying and geography.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kingston.ac.uk/faculties/science-engineering-and-computing/|title=Faculty of Engineering, Computing and the Environment|website=www.kingston.ac.uk|date=15 August 2022|language=en-GB|access-date=15 August 2022}}</ref> The faculty is based at the University's Penrhyn Road and Roehampton Vale campuses.

