==Athletics==
Kingston University London athletic teams are nicknamed the "Cougars".

==International partners==
The university holds a number of links with institutions from around the world to share teaching and research and facilitate staff and student exchanges. Kingston has a number of international 'Study Abroad' or 'Exchange' partner institutions.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/visitingstudents/studyabroad/partners/|title=International partner institutions - Study Abroad - Visiting students - Kingston University London|website=www.kingston.ac.uk}}</ref>

==Controversies==
{{criticism section|date=March 2025}}

===Humanities department closure 2025===
Kingston University has initiated the closure of its humanities department,<ref name="Kingston Axes Philosophy centre in humanities department shutdown">{{cite news|last=Grove|first=Jack|date=6 March 2025|title=Kingston axes philosophy centre in humanities department shutdown|work=Times Higher Education|url=https://www.timeshighereducation.com/news/kingston-axes-philosophy-centre-humanities-department-shutdown|access-date=14 March 2025}}</ref> including its graduate research teaching programme in philosophy, the Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy (CRMEP). In February 2024, the Head of the School of Creative and Cultural Industries advised students that it had launched a review of its philosophy programme, citing declining enrolment, lack of government funding and the Philosophy programme’s focus on post-graduate teaching as reasons.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grove |first=Jack |date=August 15, 2024 |title='Will the funding crisis confine UK research to elite universities?' |url=https://www.timeshighereducation.com/depth/will-funding-crisis-confine-uk-research-elite-universities |access-date=14 March 2025 |website=Times Higher Education}}</ref> The review was expected to complete in six weeks. The outcome of that review was not made public. On 26 February 2025, the Dean of Kingston School of Art advised students that Kingston had begun a 30 day consultation period to shut down teaching in the entirety of the humanities, to include closure of its English and philosophy programmes, stating that these programmes are inconsistent with Kingston’s new Town House strategy and future skills delivery.<ref name="Kingston Plans to Close Its Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy">{{cite news|last=Weinberg|first=Justin|date=7 March 2025|title=Kingston Plans to Close Its Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy|work=Daily Nous|url=https://dailynous.com/2025/03/07/kingston-plans-to-close-its-centre-for-research-in-modern-european-philosophy/|access-date=14 March 2025}}</ref>

''The Times Higher Education'' noted that "Kingston has also suspended applications for new students to its foundation year humanities programme “with a view to it closing in the new longer term”, according to the email sent by Kingston School of Art dean Mandy Ure."<ref name="Kingston Axes Philosophy centre in humanities department shutdown"/> The closures are also expected to affect the creative writing program and the Kingston Language Scheme which teaches modern languages.<ref name="Kingston Axes Philosophy">{{cite news|last=Grove|first=Jack|date=6 March 2025|title=Kingston axes philosophy centre in humanities department shutdown|publisher=Times Higher Education|url=https://www.timeshighereducation.com/news/kingston-axes-philosophy-centre-humanities-department-shutdown|access-date=14 March 2025}}</ref> In an open letter, published in the contemporary art forum e-Flux and the British publishing house [[Verso Books|Verso]], the CRMEP students state they "unequivocally condemn" the closures, writing that the proposals are an "affront against a broadly humanist concept of education, in which the independence of culture and research from immediate market imperatives" affirms their social value.<ref name="Open Letter from CRMEP Students & Researchers on Kingston University’s Proposed Closure of the Department of Humanities">{{citation|author=MA, MPhil, and PhD students and researchers in the Centre for Research in Modern European Philosophy, Kingston University, London |date=5 March 2025|title=Open Letter from CRMEP Students & Researchers on Kingston University's Proposed Closure of the Department of Humanities|publisher=e-Flux Notes|url=https://www.e-flux.com/notes/658933/open-letter-from-crmep-students-researchers-on-kingston-university-s-proposed-closure-of-the-department-of-humanities}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=5 March 2025 |title=Open Letter from CRMEP Students & Researchers on Kingston University's Proposed Closure of the Department of Humanities |url=https://www.versobooks.com/blogs/news/open-letter-from-crmep-students-researchers-on-kingston-university-s-proposed-closure-of-the-department-of-humanities |access-date=14 March 2025 |website=Verso}}</ref>  Kingston University's Senior Leadership Team led by the Vice-Chancellor Stephen Spier has expressed its intention to implement £20 million in savings and to lay-off staff and close programmes to take effect at the end of July.  The Kingston [[University and College Union|University College Union]] has since passed a motion of 'no-confidence' in the Senior Leadership Team.{{citation needed|date=March 2025}} 
[[File:Judith-butler-CRMEP2-March2024.jpg|thumb|alt=Judith-Butler-speaking-on Kafka-CRMEP-Feb2024|Judith Butler speaking on Kafka at CRMEP Feb 2024]]

===BMus external examiner===
In 2008, the [[BBC]] obtained e-mails circulated within Kingston's School of Music, relating to the opinions of an [[external examiner]] moderating the BMus course.<ref name="BBCDroppedCriticism">{{cite news|last=Coughlan|first=Sean|date=24 June 2008|title=Examiner dropped course criticism|publisher=BBC|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/education/7470125.stm|access-date=25 June 2008}}</ref> The messages showed that her final report caused considerable concern within the department. The examiner was persuaded to moderate her criticism following contact from a member of the university's staff. The e-mails also detailed a plan to replace her (at the end of her term) with a more experienced and broad-based external examiner, a process which Kingston stressed breaks no rules relating to the appointment of such examiners.<ref name="BBCDroppedCriticism" />

In October 2008, Peter Williams, Chief Executive of the UK Quality Assurance Agency (QAA), presented the agency's findings to a Parliamentary Select Committee charged with investigating standards in British higher education. Following an investigation of the allegations by a former University staff member that undue pressure was applied to the School of Music's External Examiner, QAA upheld all charges of wrongdoing, as alleged.<ref>{{cite web|author=The Committee Office, House of Commons|title=House of Commons - Innovation, Universities, Science and Skills - Minutes of Evidence|url=http://www.parliament.the-stationery-office.com/pa/cm200708/cmselect/cmdius/905/8071710.htm|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110716215655/http://www.parliament.the-stationery-office.com/pa/cm200708/cmselect/cmdius/905/8071710.htm|archive-date=16 July 2011|access-date=15 November 2012|publisher=Parliament.the-stationery-office.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Kingston University Special review of the circumstances surrounding the amendments to an external examiner's report|url=http://www.qaa.ac.uk/causesforconcern/kingston09.asp|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090719205943/http://www.qaa.ac.uk/causesforconcern/Kingston09.asp|archive-date=19 July 2009|access-date=25 March 2009}}</ref><ref name="THESExtExaminer">{{cite news|last=Newman|first=Melanie|date=26 March 2009|title=Kingston Showed Lack of Regard for External Examiner's Role, Says QAA|publisher=Times Higher Education|url=http://www.timeshighereducation.co.uk/story.asp?sectioncode=26&storycode=405954&c=1|access-date=27 March 2008}}</ref>

===Controversial speakers===
In 2015, Prime Minister [[David Cameron]] named and shamed four British universities which gave platforms to allegedly 'extremist' speakers.<ref>[https://www.telegraph.co.uk/education/universityeducation/11870429/British-universities-that-give-the-floor-to-extremist-speakers-are-named-and-shamed.html British universities that give the floor to extremist speakers are named and shamed], ''The Telegraph''</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title='Show us the evidence David Cameron': Supporters rally after Kingston University listed for extremism|date=30 October 2015 |url=http://www.surreycomet.co.uk/news/13924322._Show_us_the_evidence_David_Cameron___Supporters_rally_after_Kingston_University_listed_for_extremism/#comments-anchor|publisher=Surrey Comet}}</ref>

Kingston's Vice Chancellor [[Julius Weinberg]] defended his decision to allow controversial speakers in the name of free speech.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/higher-education-network/2016/feb/23/its-the-job-of-universities-to-challenge-ideas-we-cant-let-prevent-get-int-the-way I won't stop offering a platform to so-called 'hate speakers'], ''The Guardian''</ref>

===National Student Survey exaggeration===
In 2008, an audio recording obtained by student media included two psychology lecturers asking students to inflate their graded opinions given as part of the [[National Student Survey]].<ref name="BBCFakingSurvey">{{cite news|last=Coughlin|first=Sean|date=13 May 2008|title=University staff faking survey|publisher=BBC|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/education/7397979.stm|access-date=13 May 2008}}</ref> One member of staff was recorded as encouraging students to boost specific satisfaction scores, because "if Kingston comes down the bottom [of the league tables], then the bottom line is that nobody is going to want to employ you because they are going to think your degree is shit".<ref name="BBCFakingSurvey" /><ref>{{cite news|last=Mostrous|first=Alexi|date=14 May 2008|title=Kingston University students told to lie to boost college's rank in government poll|work=[[The Times]]|url=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/article3924417.ece|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080726195511/http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/article3924417.ece|url-status=dead|archive-date=26 July 2008|access-date=28 June 2008}}</ref> In response, Vice-Chancellor [[Peter Scott (educationalist)|Peter Scott]] confirmed that the recording was genuine but added that he believed that the incident was an isolated one.<ref>{{cite news|date=14 May 2008|title=Statement in response to National Student Survey complaint|publisher=Kingston University Press Office|url=http://www.kingston.ac.uk/pressoffice/latestnews/2008/may/14-Statement-in-response-to-National-Student-Survey-complaint/|access-date=2 July 2008}}</ref> In July 2008, the Higher Education Funding Council of England removed the university's Department of Psychology of the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences from the League Tables for the year as its sanction for having fraudulently manipulated the National Student Survey results.<ref name="BBCFacultyExpulsion">{{cite news|last=Coughlin|first=Sean|date=26 July 2008|title=Faculty in league table expulsion|publisher=BBC|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/education/7526061.stm|access-date=26 July 2008}}</ref>

